What it is: One commit that bundles two batches of bug fixes I'd been working on — fixing the database migration setup and fixing broken features (notifications + lead-to-deal conversion).
Part 1 — Fixed the database migration (Alembic) setup
- Alembic was totally broken — any command crashed with KeyError: 'b7a1c3e9f0d2' because a migration file (b7a1c3e9f0d2, aka "add response time accumulators") was referenced by a merge migration but the file itself had been deleted from the repo.
- Restored that file and its parent (20260730_drop_domain_events_table) from git history. Both were already applied to the live Supabase DB, so it was safe (idempotent).
- Fixed an orphaned migration: 20260723_0008 had down_revision = None but its docstring says it should chain off 20260723_0007 — fixed to match.
- De-duplicated two migrations that both claimed the revision ID 20260810_0001 — renamed the sales_targets one to 20260810_0002 (file + revision).
- After the fix: alembic heads / history / current all work again, and the DB is confirmed at e5f9c1d8a7b6.
Part 2 — Fixed notifications not firing
- Root cause: the event's actor_id was stored in the DB but never passed along when events were turned into notification envelopes, so the notification consumer looked for a field that didn't exist.
- Fix: added actor_id to the EventEnvelope, propagated it in the worker, and read it in the consumer.
- Also deleted broken duplicate subscribe/unsubscribe methods in event_bus.py that referenced a variable that didn't exist (would have crashed if called).
- Frontend: useNotifications hook now reuses a single AudioContext (instead of creating one per sound) and calls resume() so the sound works despite browser autoplay rules; shows a toast when a new notification arrives.
Part 3 — Fixed lead → deal conversion bugs
- Contact missing after conversion: the created contact was missing its owner_id, so it didn't show up in the owner's list. Now it inherits the lead's owner.
- Duplicate-company crash: converting a lead whose company had been soft-deleted crashed with an IntegrityError because the old row still occupies the unique index slot but the lookup only checked non-deleted rows. Added a repository method to find soft-deleted companies, and conversion now reuses + un-deletes them instead of crashing. Also wrapped the create in a try/except so concurrent conversions raise a clean "duplicate" error instead of a 500.
- "Untitled Deal" / "Acme Corp" bug: the pipeline view was remapping API data that was already mapped (double mapping), mangling the deal title/company/value. Fixed the field fallbacks.
- Silent conversion failure: the leads page was catching errors and falsely marking leads "Converted (Offline)" — now it shows a proper error toast.
Net result: py_compile passed for all backend files, and the frontend build compiled successfully.